<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Vladislav Janeček
SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later

The manifest of the MSIX package (build-msix.ps1, the workflow
windows-msix.yml): the unpackaged app folder of build.ps1 app, unchanged,
given a package identity, for the Microsoft Store (a release tag builds it
unsigned for Partner Center) or as a test package (docs/windows-port.md
section 17, docs/releasing.md section 7).

The tokens in double braces are filled in by build-msix.ps1.

What the package changes for the app, and what the test is for:
- AppData and HKCU stay virtualised, the MSIX default. The Store denied
  the restricted capability unvirtualizedResources (2026-11-13, policy
  10.6.3), so the package no longer declares it or the desktop6
  FileSystemWriteVirtualization and RegistryWriteVirtualization switches.
  The app, its daemon and the bridge all run in the package and see the
  same virtualised %LOCALAPPDATA%\Malachi Mail\ and HKCU; the socket and
  its key (%USERPROFILE%\.cache\malachi\run\) and Claude Code's
  ~/.claude.json lie outside AppData and are not virtualised. Measured
  (docs/windows-port.md section 17): on an account that had the unpackaged
  app, files go on to the real folder, which exists, and only new HKCU
  values go to the package's registry; on a clean account the data and
  the preferences live in the package's own store alone, which no program
  outside the package sees and which Windows removes with the package.
- What programs outside the package must reach therefore lies outside
  AppData (Paths.OutsideAppData): the working directory of the assistant
  (Claude Code could not enter the virtualised one) and the attachments
  written for opening, both under %USERPROFILE%\.cache\malachi\.
- The app notifications: a packaged app's AppNotificationManager.Register()
  takes its COM class from this manifest (ToastActivatorCLSID and the
  ExeServer below; the arguments are the ones the unpackaged registration
  uses). The GUID is this package's own, never reused elsewhere.
- The bridge's app execution alias (uap3, malachi-mcp.exe in
  %LOCALAPPDATA%\Microsoft\WindowsApps): Windows lets no process outside
  the package start an executable in its folder (Claude Code and Claude
  Desktop got EPERM), but anyone may start the alias, which keeps its path
  across updates. The app hands it out in a package (Paths.InPackage).
- mailto: (windows.protocol): Windows lists
  the package in Default Apps under a ProgID of its own and drops it with
  the package; the app in a package writes no mailto: keys of its own
  (they named the versioned package folder and went stale with every
  update) and removes the ones an earlier test package wrote
  (MailtoRegistration.IsFromAPackage). A link arrives as a Protocol
  activation (Program.RequestOf).
- The start at sign-in (uap5, windows.startupTask): a Run value written
  in the package would land in the package's registry, which Windows does
  not read at sign-in. The task is off until the user turns Launch at
  Login on in Preferences (PackagedLaunchAtLogin over
  Windows.ApplicationModel.StartupTask; the TaskId is
  PackageStartupTask.DefaultTaskId), Windows lists it under Settings >
  Apps > Startup, and its start arrives as a StartupTask activation,
  which the app takes as the unpackaged app's start in the background
  (Program.RequestOf). The app removes the Run value an earlier test
  package wrote (LaunchAtLogin.RemoveOfAPackage).
-->
<Package
  xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/foundation/windows10"
  xmlns:uap="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/uap/windows10"
  xmlns:uap3="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/uap/windows10/3"
  xmlns:uap5="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/uap/windows10/5"
  xmlns:rescap="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/foundation/windows10/restrictedcapabilities"
  xmlns:desktop="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/desktop/windows10"
  xmlns:com="http://schemas.microsoft.com/appx/manifest/com/windows10"
  IgnorableNamespaces="uap uap3 uap5 rescap desktop com">

  <Identity
    Name="{{IdentityName}}"
    Publisher="{{Publisher}}"
    Version="{{Version}}"
    ProcessorArchitecture="{{Arch}}" />

  <Properties>
    <DisplayName>Malachi Mail</DisplayName>
    <PublisherDisplayName>{{PublisherDisplayName}}</PublisherDisplayName>
    <Logo>Assets\Package\StoreLogo.png</Logo>
  </Properties>

  <Dependencies>
    <!-- Windows 11, as the unpackaged app (windows/README.md). The Windows
         App SDK is inside the app folder (self-contained), so there is no
         framework dependency. -->
    <TargetDeviceFamily Name="Windows.Desktop" MinVersion="10.0.22000.0" MaxVersionTested="10.0.26100.0" />
  </Dependencies>

  <!-- The languages the Store lists for the app: English and every
       language of po/LINGUAS, written here by build-msix.ps1, so a new
       translation reaches the package without an edit of this file. -->
  <Resources>
{{Resources}}
  </Resources>

  <Applications>
    <Application Id="MalachiMail" Executable="MalachiMail.exe" EntryPoint="Windows.FullTrustApplication">
      <uap:VisualElements
        DisplayName="Malachi Mail"
        Description="Malachi Mail"
        BackgroundColor="transparent"
        Square150x150Logo="Assets\Package\Square150x150Logo.png"
        Square44x44Logo="Assets\Package\Square44x44Logo.png" />
      <Extensions>
        <desktop:Extension Category="windows.toastNotificationActivation">
          <desktop:ToastNotificationActivation ToastActivatorCLSID="6F1B2C4A-8E37-4D5B-9A21-3C7E5F0D8B64" />
        </desktop:Extension>
        <uap:Extension Category="windows.protocol">
          <uap:Protocol Name="mailto">
            <uap:DisplayName>Malachi Mail</uap:DisplayName>
          </uap:Protocol>
        </uap:Extension>
        <uap3:Extension Category="windows.appExecutionAlias" Executable="malachi-mcp.exe" EntryPoint="Windows.FullTrustApplication">
          <uap3:AppExecutionAlias>
            <desktop:ExecutionAlias Alias="malachi-mcp.exe" />
          </uap3:AppExecutionAlias>
        </uap3:Extension>
        <uap5:Extension Category="windows.startupTask" Executable="MalachiMail.exe" EntryPoint="Windows.FullTrustApplication">
          <uap5:StartupTask TaskId="MalachiMailStartup" Enabled="false" DisplayName="Malachi Mail" />
        </uap5:Extension>
        <com:Extension Category="windows.comServer">
          <com:ComServer>
            <com:ExeServer Executable="MalachiMail.exe" DisplayName="Malachi Mail" Arguments="----AppNotificationActivated:">
              <com:Class Id="6F1B2C4A-8E37-4D5B-9A21-3C7E5F0D8B64" />
            </com:ExeServer>
          </com:ComServer>
        </com:Extension>
      </Extensions>
    </Application>
  </Applications>

  <Capabilities>
    <rescap:Capability Name="runFullTrust" />
  </Capabilities>
</Package>
